      nanos: 949487922
    }
  }
  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Shifting Focus, Again**\n\nI\'ve hit a temporary wall with the image generation; quota issues, as expected. I\'m pivoting to vector-based imagery for now. It\'s a natural shift, and I\'m excited about the possibilities of using vectors. Let\'s see what amazing things can be done.\n\n\n**Exploring Procedural Generation**\n\nMy focus has shifted to procedural steel defect generation using SVG or HTML Canvas on the frontend. This approach offers speed, reliability (no quotas!), and dynamic, high-resolution textures. We can simulate scratches, fissures, and pitting using code. I\'m excited about pairing this with Gemini 3.5 Flash for user-uploaded images, providing deep-learning-like detection results.\n\n\n**Integrating Procedural Images**\n\nI\'ve made a breakthrough! Clicking procedural samples lets us capture the canvas image as a base64 DataURL, sending it seamlessly to the `/api/detect` endpoint. This unifies our system, allowing pre-loaded and user-uploaded images to leverage the same Gemini AI detection stack. It\'s a fantastic demonstration of computer vision, directly testing its ability to analyze procedurally generated textures!\n\n\n**Defining Defect Detection**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on defining the core defect categories and structure for the JSON response. The goal is to accurately identify and categorize defects like scratches, cracks, pitting, inclusions, scale, and patches, while calculating severity, density, and overall status. The structured output will be crucial for the quality inspection system.\n\n\n**Refining JSON Output Format**\n\nI\'m finalizing the JSON response schema for defect detection. It will contain detailed information on defects like inclusions, scale, and patches, providing Chinese descriptions, severity levels, bounding box coordinates (normalized 0-100), and confidence scores. It\'ll also have metallurgical explanations and recommended actions in Chinese. I\'m focusing on defining the response schema fully, to ensure it is compliant.\n\n\n**Structuring the Data Output**\n\nI\'m now establishing the main JSON output structure for defect analysis. This will include an `overallStatus` string (Pass/Fail/Marginal), a `severityIndex` integer (0-100), and a `defectDensity` percentage. The core data will be in a `defects` array, describing each flaw with details like type, Chinese name, short description, and bounding box coordinates for precise localization.\n\n\n**Defining Defect Details**\n\nI\'m now detailing each defect category within the JSON output. I will include `severity` levels (Low, Medium, High), bounding box coordinates normalized to 0-100, and confidence scores. Each defect will also be explained with metallurgical details in Chinese and have recommended actions. This schema ensures the JSON structure is comprehensive.\n\n\n**Defining Data Structures**\n\nI\'ve got the ideal JSON format ready, ensuring seamless parsing on the frontend. I\'m now documenting the existing Tailwind CSS configuration (v4), checking `index.html` and `src/App.tsx` for relevant styles. I\'m structuring directories and I\'ll create `src/types.ts` for defect data, `server.ts` for the Express server (handling `/api/detect`), and `src/data/samples.ts` for procedural defect generation.\n\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will create a Types file (`/src/types.ts`) to establish a formal data structure for defect categories, detection results, and historical inspection records. This forms the interface backbone of our multi-module industrial inspection system."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 17
      nanos: 91279703
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
